INSERT INTO `reading_history` VALUES (1,36,'en','The Evolution of Communication: From Signals to Satellites','B2-C1',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,3,3,'2026-07-04 10:36:14'),(2,36,'en','The Unseen Hand of Historiography','advanced',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,2,3,'2026-07-04 10:40:11'),(3,36,'en','The Quiet Revolution: How AI Is Reshaping Professional Work','Advanced (C1)',NULL,'Artificial intelligence is no longer a distant concept confined to science fiction. It has become an integral part of the modern workplace, quietly transforming how professionals across industries approach their daily tasks. Far from the dystopian narratives of mass unemployment, the current wave of AI integration is more about augmentation than replacement. For knowledge workers—from lawyers and doctors to marketers and software engineers—AI tools are emerging as powerful collaborators, enhancing efficiency and unlocking new realms of possibility. Consider the field of data analysis. Traditionally, sifting through terabytes of unstructured data to find actionable insights was a laborious process that could take weeks. Now, with advanced machine learning algorithms, professionals can deploy models that not only process information at an unprecedented speed but also identify patterns that might elude the human eye. This synergy allows experts to focus on strategic decision-making rather than mundane data sorting. Similarly, in the legal profession, AI-driven document review systems can scan thousands of contracts in minutes, flagging anomalous clauses with a level of accuracy that rivals, and sometimes surpasses, that of a human associate. However, this transformation demands a new set of skills. The professional of tomorrow must be digitally literate, understanding not just how to use AI tools but also their underlying logic and inherent biases. A doctor using an AI diagnostic assistant, for instance, must know when to trust its recommendation and when to question it based on a patient’s unique history. This critical thinking is crucial because AI models are only as good as the data they are trained on; if the data contains systemic biases, the AI’s output will likely perpetuate those disparities. The ethical dimension is equally important. As businesses adopt AI for tasks like resume screening or loan approvals, the question of accountability comes to the fore. If an algorithm inadvertently discriminates against a certain group, who is responsible—the developer, the company, or the machine itself? These are not mere hypotheticals; they are pressing realities that professionals must navigate. In response, many organizations are establishing ethics boards to oversee AI deployment, ensuring that human values remain at the center of technological progress. Ultimately, the rise of AI in the professional sphere is not a sprint but a long-term evolution. It offers the promise of freeing up human ingenuity from repetitive tasks, allowing us to tackle more creative and complex problems. Yet, it also requires us to be vigilant, adaptive, and ethically conscious. For the modern professional, embracing AI is not optional; it is essential for staying relevant in a rapidly changing world.','[{\"word\":\"augmentation\",\"phonetic\":\"/ˌɔːɡmənˈteɪʃən/\",\"definition\":\"增强；强化\"},{\"word\":\"unprecedented\",\"phonetic\":\"/ʌnˈpresɪdentɪd/\",\"definition\":\"史无前例的；空前的\"},{\"word\":\"anomalous\",\"phonetic\":\"/əˈnɒmələs/\",\"definition\":\"异常的；反常的\"},{\"word\":\"perpetuate\",\"phonetic\":\"/pərˈpetʃueɪt/\",\"definition\":\"使持久；使持续\"},{\"word\":\"accountability\",\"phonetic\":\"/əˌkaʊntəˈbɪləti/\",\"definition\":\"责任制；问责\"}]','[{\"question\":\"According to the article, what is the primary way AI is currently affecting professional work?\",\"options\":[\"A. It is replacing most human jobs in data analysis.\",\"B. It serves as a collaborator to enhance human capabilities.\",\"C. It has made critical thinking unnecessary for professionals.\",\"D. It mainly creates ethical problems without offering benefits.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explanation\":\"The article states that \'the current wave of AI integration is more about augmentation than replacement\' and describes AI tools as \'powerful collaborators,\' which aligns with option B.\"},{\"question\":\"What is a key risk mentioned regarding AI models in professional settings?\",\"options\":[\"A. They always process data too slowly.\",\"B. They can only be used by software engineers.\",\"C. They may contain and reinforce biases from the training data.\",\"D. They are too expensive for most businesses to adopt.\"],\"answer\":2,\"explanation\":\"The text explicitly warns that \'AI models are only as good as the data they are trained on; if the data contains systemic biases, the AI’s output will likely perpetuate those disparities,\' making option C correct.\"},{\"question\":\"What does the article suggest professionals need to do to adapt to AI in the workplace?\",\"options\":[\"A. Avoid using AI tools to maintain job security.\",\"B. Develop digital literacy and critical thinking skills.\",\"C. Rely solely on AI recommendations without question.\",\"D. Focus only on repetitive tasks that AI cannot handle.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explanation\":\"The article emphasizes that professionals must be \'digitally literate\' and exercise \'critical thinking\' when using AI, such as knowing when to trust or question a diagnostic assistant, which supports option B.\"}]',2,3,'2026-07-04 10:55:21'),(4,1,'en','The Rise of Edge Computing: Transforming Data Processing','Upper-Intermediate to Advanced',NULL,'In recent years, the proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) devices has generated an unprecedented volume of data. Traditional cloud computing, which relies on centralized data centers, is struggling to keep pace with the demands for low latency and real-time processing. This has given rise to a paradigm shift known as edge computing. Unlike cloud computing, where data is sent to a remote server for analysis, edge computing processes data closer to its source—at the \'edge\' of the network, such as on local devices or nearby servers. \n\nEdge computing offers several compelling advantages. First, it significantly reduces latency because data does not need to travel long distances to a central cloud. This is critical for applications like autonomous vehicles, where milliseconds can mean the difference between safety and disaster. Second, it enhances bandwidth efficiency by filtering and processing data locally, sending only relevant information to the cloud. This alleviates network congestion and reduces operational costs. Third, edge computing improves data privacy and security. Sensitive information can be processed locally without being transmitted across vulnerable networks, which is particularly valuable in healthcare and finance. \n\nHowever, the adoption of edge computing is not without challenges. Managing a distributed network of edge devices requires robust orchestration and security protocols. Each device represents a potential entry point for cyberattacks, and ensuring consistent updates across thousands of nodes is complex. Additionally, edge devices often have limited computational power and storage, which can constrain the complexity of applications they can run. \n\nDespite these hurdles, the trajectory of edge computing is promising. According to a report by Gartner, by 2025, 75% of enterprise-generated data will be processed outside of traditional centralized data centers—at the edge. Major tech companies like Amazon, Microsoft, and Google are investing heavily in edge computing frameworks, such as AWS Wavelength and Azure Edge Zones. These platforms aim to bring cloud services closer to users, enabling new use cases in augmented reality, smart cities, and industrial automation. \n\nFor professionals in the tech industry, understanding edge computing is no longer optional. It is reshaping how we think about network architecture, software deployment, and data governance. As connectivity expands with 5G networks, the synergy between edge computing and 5G will unlock even greater possibilities. Low latency and high bandwidth from 5G will empower edge devices to handle real-time analytics, making the network itself more intelligent. \n\nIn conclusion, edge computing represents a fundamental evolution in how data is processed. It addresses the limitations of cloud computing in an increasingly connected world. While challenges remain, its potential to drive innovation in critical sectors makes it a key area for professional development. Embracing this shift will be essential for staying competitive in the digital age.','[{\"word\":\"proliferation\",\"phonetic\":\"/prəˌlɪf.əˈreɪ.ʃən/\",\"definition\":\"激增，扩散\"},{\"word\":\"latency\",\"phonetic\":\"/ˈleɪ.tən.si/\",\"definition\":\"延迟，时延\"},{\"word\":\"alleviate\",\"phonetic\":\"/əˈliː.vi.eɪt/\",\"definition\":\"减轻，缓解\"},{\"word\":\"orchestration\",\"phonetic\":\"/ˌɔːr.kɪˈstreɪ.ʃən/\",\"definition\":\"协调，精心安排\"},{\"word\":\"trajectory\",\"phonetic\":\"/trəˈdʒek.tər.i/\",\"definition\":\"轨迹，发展趋势\"}]','[{\"question\":\"What is the primary difference between edge computing and traditional cloud computing?\",\"options\":[\"Edge computing relies solely on centralized data centers.\",\"Edge computing processes data closer to its source rather than in a central cloud.\",\"Edge computing is slower than cloud computing due to local processing.\",\"Edge computing requires no network connectivity.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explanation\":\"The article states that edge computing processes data at the \'edge\' of the network, near the data source, unlike cloud computing which sends data to remote centralized servers.\"},{\"question\":\"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an advantage of edge computing?\",\"options\":[\"Reducing latency for real-time applications.\",\"Improving bandwidth efficiency by filtering data locally.\",\"Enhancing data security by limiting transmission over networks.\",\"Increasing the computational power of all connected devices.\"],\"answer\":3,\"explanation\":\"The article lists reducing latency, improving bandwidth efficiency, and enhancing security as advantages, but notes that edge devices often have limited computational power.\"},{\"question\":\"What challenge does the article highlight regarding edge computing adoption?\",\"options\":[\"Edge devices are too expensive to deploy.\",\"Managing security across numerous distributed edge devices is complex.\",\"Edge computing cannot handle real-time data processing.\",\"Cloud computing is more reliable than edge computing.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explanation\":\"The article mentions that managing a distributed network of edge devices requires robust security protocols, and each device is a potential entry point for cyberattacks.\"},{\"question\":\"According to the Gartner report mentioned, what percentage of enterprise-generated data will be processed at the edge by 2025?\",\"options\":[\"50%\",\"60%\",\"75%\",\"90%\"],\"answer\":2,\"explanation\":\"The article explicitly states: \'by 2025, 75% of enterprise-generated data will be processed outside of traditional centralized data centers—at the edge.\'\"},{\"question\":\"How does 5G technology relate to edge computing, according to the article?\",\"options\":[\"5G replaces the need for edge computing entirely.\",\"5G and edge computing work together to achieve lower latency and higher bandwidth.\",\"5G networks make edge computing obsolete.\",\"5G only supports cloud computing, not edge computing.\"],\"answer\":1,\"explanation\":\"The article notes that \'the synergy between edge computing and 5G will unlock even greater possibilities,\' with low latency and high bandwidth from 5G empowering edge devices.\"}]',1,5,'2026-07-04 11:53:28');
